{"text":"Recent work supports combining sequential collaborative signals with item text, but fusion design matters. [TedRec (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.18166) argues that directly adding text and ID embeddings can be ineffective; it instead uses Fourier-domain operations for sequence-level fusion. [ITS-Rec (2025)](https://www.mdpi.com/2079-9292/14/9/1748) incorporates BERT representations of titles, descriptions and reviews. Its pretrained encoder and review inputs prevent direct adoption under this benchmark’s constraints. These papers motivate content integration, without establishing gains for a TF-IDF hybrid trained from scratch.

A relevant foundation is [LightFM](https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.08439): jointly learned metadata-feature embeddings support sparse and cold-start recommendation without requiring pretrained language weights, although the model itself is not sequential. A practical adaptation would encode available titles/descriptions with sparse word and character TF-IDF, then learn a low-dimensional projection alongside SASRec. Alternatively, blend SASRec scores with cosine similarity between candidate text and a recency-weighted history text profile. These are proposed adaptations, not reproduced findings from the cited papers. Separate branches permit testing content contributions before committing to more expensive sequence-level fusion.

For the four-category benchmark, compare ID-only, text-only and hybrid variants using identical validation candidates and macro HR@10. Tune a bounded fusion weight per category before introducing a learned gate based on training interaction counts, history length and text availability. Lexical overlap may distinguish artists, formats and equipment identifiers, but cannot establish compatibility when specifications are absent. Treat missing evidence as unknown; use only supplied histories, titles, descriptions and timestamps. Any cold-start benefit or overall improvement requires validation across all four categories."}
